            "DetailedDescription": "<!--\n  ~ //-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  ~ //  Copyright (c) 2026 Basler AG\n  ~ //-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  -->\n\n<h2><strong>Demultiplexer</strong></h2>\n\n<p>\n    The Demultiplexer vTool allows you to send incoming data items (updates) from one input source to one or several output pins. The data type of the <strong>Data</strong> input pin is generic. The output pins adopt the data type of the input provided. The Demultiplexer is a data-processing vTool, meaning that input and output updates are aligned.\n</p>\n<p>\n    There are the following options to configure the Demultiplexer:\n    <ul>\n    <li><strong>Operating Mode:</strong> This determines the manner in which incoming data items are output.</li>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Blockwise:</strong> Incoming data items are collected and output together at the same time. The individual items are held in slots until the specified number of data items has been collected. When this number has been reached, the data is sent to the respective output pins. Use the <strong>Number Of Slots</strong> parameter to specify how many data items you want to collect.</li>\n        <li><strong>Continuous:</strong> Each incoming data item triggers the output of data from all filled slots, including the current one. This means that when the first data item arrives and fills the first slot, it is output immediately via the corresponding output pin. When the second data item arrives, the first item and the second item are output together, and so on. Use the <strong>Number Of Slots</strong> parameter to specify how many data items you want to collect.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <li><strong>Output Mode:</strong> This determines whether incoming data items are output individually or as an array.</li>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Individual:</strong> Incoming data items are output individually. There is a dedicated output pin for each incoming data item. The <strong>Number Of Slots</strong> parameter value determines the number of output pins.</li>\n        <li><strong>Array:</strong> Incoming data items are output as an array. There is one output pin holding all data items. If the data type of the <strong>Data</strong> input pin is an array, this mode is not available.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <li><strong>Number Of Slots:</strong> Use this parameter to specify the number of slots, i.e., the desired number of data items you want to collect and output. In <strong>Individual</strong> output mode, this is also the number of output pins created. In <strong>Array</strong> output mode, this is the number of items in the array.</li>\n    <li><strong>Offset:</strong> By specifying an offset you can shift incoming data items to the desired slot and corresponding output pin. The Demultiplexer uses an internal counter to route data items to slots. The internal counter is reset to 0 when the recipe is started. If the <strong>Offset</strong> parameter is set to 0, incoming data item 1 is sent to slot 1, data item 2 is sent to slot 2, and so on. If you specify an offset, this value is added to the internal counter value.<br><strong>Example:</strong> You have 3 slots configured and specify an offset of 1. This would send the data item that would have gone to slot/output pin 1 to slot/output pin 2. Data item 2 would go to slot/output pin 3. Data item 3 wraps around and would go to slot/output pin 1.<br>This wrapping behavior also occurs if the offset value is higher than the number of slots configured.</li>\n    </ul>\n</p>\n<p>\n    You can also use the <strong>Index</strong> input pin to override the internal counter and route incoming data items to the desired output pins. The same wrapping of data occurs as described above for the <strong>Offset</strong> parameter. If the <strong>Index</strong> pin isn't connected or no input is received, the internal counter is used. If the <strong>Index</strong> pin has an error and input is received at the <strong>Data</strong> pin in the same update, that input is discarded.\n</p>\n<p><br></p>\n<h3><strong>Inputs</strong></h3>\n<p>\n    <strong>Data:</strong> Accepts input of any data type.\n</p>\n<p>\n    <strong>Index:</strong> Accepts integer input for overriding the internal counter. \n</p>\n<p><br></p>\n<h3><strong>Outputs</strong></h3>\n<p>\n    <strong>DataN:</strong> Returns the input held in the corresponding slot.\n</p>\n<p>\n    <strong>Array:</strong> Returns the data items received in one array.\n</p>\n<p><br></p>\n<h3><strong>Configuring the vTool</strong></h3>\n<p>\n  Double-click the vTool to open a settings dialog. This is a graphical representation of the vTool's parameters.\n</p>\n<p>\n  You can also configure the vTool via the pylon API and the <strong>Features - All</strong> pane of the pylon Viewer.\n  All parameters listed there can be set via the pylon API. To study the effects of a parameter, change the parameter\n  values in the <strong>Features - All</strong> pane and watch the results in a pin data view when running the recipe.\n  For more details about parameters, select a parameter and check the <strong>Feature Documentation</strong> pane.\n</p>\n<p>\n  <a href=\"pylonVToolDemultiplexer\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Get help</u></a>\n</p>\n",
